less intelligent people didn't understand the themes and nuances of his work. Perhaps you could be more specific and let us know which words you didn't understand, he said to someone who had commented that the author loves using big words, but hates using them correctly. Another reader commented on what they considered odd word choices, like referring to a bulldog as a tenacious tyke that has talon-like claws. They accused the author of overusing a thesaurus and called it the sort of writing they would expect from a precocious young teen eager to impress his English teacher. Richard responded, When I won countdown, I got a 20 volume dictionary but have never owned a thesaurus.

